{"answer":"There are 7 subsets:\n{1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {1,2,6}, {4,5}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.\n\nFor example, {1,8} has sum 9. Partition every nonempty candidate subset by its largest element m. If m=8,7,6,5,4, the remaining required sums are respectively 1,2,3,4,5; enumerating distinct smaller parts gives the displayed subsets. If m≤3, even 1+2+3=6<9, so there are none. The largest-element cases are mutually exclusive and cover every nonempty subset, so the count is exhaustive.","approaches":[{"description":"Partition subsets by their largest element, then enumerate the distinct smaller elements needed to make the remaining sum.","id":"largest-partition","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The maximum of a nonempty finite subset is unique, making the cases disjoint and exhaustive."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question specifies the ground set {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} and target sum 9.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"given-instance","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Candidate subsets are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}, and the required element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the largest-element enumeration in steps partition-max through count-total.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-instance"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"subset-count","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have element-sum 9.","step_ids":["partition-max","enumerate-cases","exclude-small-max","count-total"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Listed and checked in enumerate-cases.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["given-instance"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is one requested subset, since 1+8=9.","step_ids":["enumerate-cases"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"partition-max","justification":"Every nonempty finite subset has one largest element m. Since all elements are positive and the total is 9, m is in {1,...,8}; hence these cases are mutually exclusive and cover all candidates.","statement":"Partition candidates according to their largest element m."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["partition-max"],"id":"enumerate-cases","justification":"For each m, the other elements must be distinct members smaller than m with sum 9-m. The possibilities are: m=8: {1}; m=7: {2}; m=6: {3} or {1,2}; m=5: {4} or {1,3}; m=4: {2,3}. Thus the corresponding full subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {1,2,6}, {4,5}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","statement":"Enumerate all candidates in the cases m=8,7,6,5,4."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["partition-max"],"id":"exclude-small-max","justification":"If m≤3, every element of the subset is at most 3, so its sum is at most 1+2+3=6, which is less than 9.","statement":"No candidate has largest element at most 3."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["enumerate-cases","exclude-small-max"],"id":"count-total","justification":"The enumeration contains 1+1+2+2+1=7 subsets for m=8,7,6,5,4 respectively, and the remaining cases contain none. By partition-max, no subset is duplicated or omitted.","statement":"The exhaustive total is 7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
